# Xiaomi Pad 5 (nabu) configuration fragment, merged on top of the upstream
# arm64 defconfig and nixpkgs' common kernel configuration.
#
# This kernel tracks nixpkgs' `linux_latest` (see ../default.nix), so it keeps
# nixpkgs' stock configuration machinery: `enableCommonConfig` and
# autoModules both stay on.  That combination is what the nixpkgs common
# config is written for — turning autoModules off makes kconfig hide every
# option whose parent ends up as a module and the common config then loses
# over a hundred settings (nftables family tables among them), so it is not a
# safe way to get a smaller kernel here. What is left for this fragment is
# what a tablet needs to decide differently.
#
# Syntax (see the generator, pkgs/os-specific/linux/kernel/generate-config.pl):
#
#   "<SYMBOL> <value>"   hard requirement
#   "<SYMBOL>? <value>"  the answer may end up different (unused option,
#                        unsatisfied dependencies, kconfig coercion)
#
# Never answer "y" for a tristate driver: a tristate can only be "m" when one
# of its dependencies is a module, kconfig then repeats the question, and the
# generator treats a repeated question as fatal.

# --- Tablet sizing ----------------------------------------------------------
# nixpkgs' common config pins 384 CPUs; the device has 8.
NR_CPUS 8
# The source is a tarball without git metadata; pinning keeps the release
# ("7.2.3") independent of how the tree was obtained, which NixOS' module
# directories and the ESP image depend on.
LOCALVERSION_AUTO n

# --- BPF type information ---------------------------------------------------
# Keep nixpkgs' common-config DWARF and BTF settings. BPF CO-RE programs need
# /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux, which is generated from DWARF by pahole. The common
# config also enables BPF_SYSCALL, BPF_JIT and BPF-dependent facilities such
# as sched_ext and the BPF qdisc. The resolved settings are checked in
# required-nabu.config so this support cannot silently disappear.

# --- Text console -----------------------------------------------------------
# The nabu DTB has no simple-framebuffer node, so the kernel must attach fbcon
# to tty0 to show the early boot log on the panel.  These are bools and
# nixpkgs' common config already answers them "y"; they are listed so a
# regression in that area is a kconfig error here rather than a black screen.
CONSOLE_TRANSLATIONS? y
SYSFB? y
FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE? y
FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE_ROTATION? y
DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION? y

# --- Post-mortem logging ----------------------------------------------------
# The nabu device tree has a ramoops region; keep the pstore backends built in
# so a boot that dies before the root filesystem is mounted still leaves its
# console log behind, readable from the next (working) boot as
# /sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0.  nixpkgs' common config builds PSTORE_RAM
# as a module and leaves the console/pmsg backends off, which makes such a
# failure completely invisible on this device (there is no serial console).
PSTORE? y
PSTORE_RAM? y
PSTORE_CONSOLE? y
PSTORE_PMSG? y

# --- Boot-chain parity with the known-good kernel ---------------------------
# The downstream kernel explicitly disables EFI_ZBOOT (its defconfig has a
# "HACK: unset CONFIG_EFI_ZBOOT"), and nixpkgs installs the plain `Image` on
# the ESP either way; keep it off so this kernel boots the same way as the one
# that currently works.  nixpkgs' common config then leaves KERNEL_ZSTD
# (compressed EFI zboot payload) without a prompt, hence the explicit answer.
EFI_ZBOOT? n
KERNEL_ZSTD? n

# --- MediaTek SPI controller -------------------------------------------------
# The downstream nt36523 touchscreen driver carries MTK-only SPI code paths
# behind #ifdef CONFIG_SPI_MT65XX (it uses that controller's private
# `struct mtk_chip_config`).  nabu is a Qualcomm board and nixpkgs' common
# config enables the MTK driver, so without this the driver does not compile;
# the 6.17 fork kernel disables it for the same reason.
SPI_MT65XX? n

# --- Devices patched in by this kernel --------------------------------------
# Everything else the device needs (SoC clock/pinctrl/interconnect, UFS, DRM
# MSM, ATH10K and friends, USB, filesystems) is already enabled by nixpkgs'
# common config plus the "answer m" default.  These symbols only exist after
# the patches in ../patches are applied, so list them explicitly to make sure
# the drivers the device tree references are actually built.
TOUCHSCREEN_NT36523_SPI? m
DRM_PANEL_NOVATEK_NT36523? m
BACKLIGHT_KTZ8866? m
SND_SOC_SM8150? m
SND_SOC_CS35L41_I2C? m
BATTERY_QCOM_FG? m
CHARGER_IDTP9418? m
